Beside the coast road at Tamarin lie the Salines de Tamarin, a patchwork of shallow basins covering some 30 hectares. Seawater was pumped in and left to evaporate under the sun of the west coast until only salt remained, a method that dates back to the French colonial period.

For a long time this was the island's main source of salt. Much of the production has since stopped in the face of cheaper imported salt, but the basins remain a striking sight, especially in the low light of late afternoon.

## Good to know

- The salt pans are a working or private site, not a park: look from the roadside unless you have been invited in.
